From f0309b5cbd1cd1d9f3e8ce5eb1e47a0f7b780a25 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 12 Sep 2026 14:26:18 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] Keep the field rail drifting: yield by position, not by
 input events

The rail was standing still in practice, for three reasons that all looked like
"the loop does not work":

- It paused on hover. On a desktop the pointer rests over the middle of the
  page, which is exactly where the rail is, so the drift never started.
- A plain vertical page scroll passing over the rail fired `wheel` on it and
  was read as the visitor moving the rail, suppressing the drift for another
  1.6s each time.
- Clicking or dragging the rail focused it, and the focus hold then stopped the
  drift for good.

Yielding is now decided by the rail's own position: if `scrollLeft` moved since
the drift last wrote it, something other than the drift moved it, so stand off
for 2.2s. That reacts to a real drag, trackpad swipe or arrow key and ignores
vertical page scrolling entirely. The focus hold is `:focus-visible` only, so a
keyboard visit holds it and a click does not. Hover no longer holds it at all.

Verified in Chromium: the rail keeps drifting with the pointer parked in the
middle of it and after a vertical page scroll over it, stops on a horizontal
wheel, and resumes once the stand-off expires. Loop still seamless (mean pixel
difference 0 at x and x + loop) at 1440 LTR, 1440 RTL and 390, and reduced
motion still stops it and restores snapping.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
